body:
The cause is that the modifier's process sets a flag via `_make_dirty()`, and `set_bone_pose()` calls `_make_dirty()`.

However, `_make_dirty()` is used in quite a few places, such as skin changes and `add_bone()`. Even in Manual mode, it's questionable whether there are cases where the modifier isn't recalculated in those situations.

If you don't want to calculate in that case, `_make_dirty()` should be implemented as follows.

```cpp
void Skeleton3D::_make_dirty() {
	if (dirty) {
		return;
	}
	dirty = true;
	_update_deferred((modifiers.is_empty() || modifier_callback_mode_process == MODIFIER_CALLBACK_MODE_PROCESS_MANUAL) ? UPDATE_FLAG_POSE : (UpdateFlag)(UPDATE_FLAG_POSE | UPDATE_FLAG_MODIFIER));
}
```

We assume the flag is set by `advance()`, so `_make_dirty()` basically doesn't need to set the flag on the Modifier.

```cpp
void Skeleton3D::_make_dirty() {
	if (dirty) {
		return;
	}
	dirty = true;
	_update_deferred(UPDATE_FLAG_POSE);
}
```

But if the issue only occurs during animation playback, specifically when `set_bone_pose()` is called, we should add an argument and call `_make_dirty(true)` within `set_bone_pose()`.

```cpp
void Skeleton3D::_make_dirty(bool p_except_modifiers = false) {
	if (dirty) {
		return;
	}
	dirty = true;
	_update_deferred((modifiers.is_empty() || p_except_modifiers) ? UPDATE_FLAG_POSE : (UpdateFlag)(UPDATE_FLAG_POSE | UPDATE_FLAG_MODIFIER));
}

void Skeleton3D::set_bone_pose_rotation(int p_bone, const Quaternion &p_rotation) {
	const int bone_size = bones.size();
	ERR_FAIL_INDEX(p_bone, bone_size);

	bones[p_bone].pose_rotation = p_rotation;
	bones[p_bone].pose_cache_dirty = true;
	if (is_inside_tree()) {
		_make_dirty(true);
		_make_bone_global_pose_subtree_dirty(p_bone);
	}
}
```

However, this approach introduces problems. For example, if the pose changes at the exact moment of pausing, Skeleton3D's `_process()` will not be called. In that case, the modifier calculations would skip an entire frame, which should be problematic. Therefore, it is generally necessary to monitor pose changes.

In conclusion, the solution will likely be either the first approach or a combination of both methods described below.

```cpp
void Skeleton3D::_make_dirty(bool p_except_modifiers = false) {
	if (dirty) {
		return;
	}
	dirty = true;
	_update_deferred((modifiers.is_empty() || p_except_modifiers) ? UPDATE_FLAG_POSE : (UpdateFlag)(UPDATE_FLAG_POSE | UPDATE_FLAG_MODIFIER));
}

void Skeleton3D::set_bone_pose_rotation(int p_bone, const Quaternion &p_rotation) {
	const int bone_size = bones.size();
	ERR_FAIL_INDEX(p_bone, bone_size);

	bones[p_bone].pose_rotation = p_rotation;
	bones[p_bone].pose_cache_dirty = true;
	if (is_inside_tree()) {
		_make_dirty(modifier_callback_mode_process == MODIFIER_CALLBACK_MODE_PROCESS_MANUAL); // <- true only when Manual.
		_make_bone_global_pose_subtree_dirty(p_bone);
	}
}
```
